course: dictionary
lesson: 6641
title: One spelling, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words look the same but mean different things. The word nu is one of them. Its meaning depends on where you find it: in a maths or science book, in a Greek alphabet lesson, in a story, or in a brand name. The words around it are the context, and they tell you which meaning is right.
explain: In a Greek alphabet lesson you might see «alpha, beta, … mu, nu, xi». Here nu is a letter, not a word to say. In a story, a grandparent might say «nu!» to hurry you along. The context shows which meaning the writer means.
explain: Be careful with the small letter ν. It looks like a small v, so it is easy to mix them up. Look at the whole line before you decide what it means.
fact: Nu is the 13th letter of the Greek alphabet.
fact: The capital letter is Ν, and the small letter is ν.
fact: Nu sounds like the English letter «n».
fact: In Yiddish, «nu» is an informal word meaning «well?», «so?» or «come on», and it is heard in some Jewish families' English.
fact: «Nu!» means well? or come on!, hurrying a friend. Said with a long sigh, it means «oh, come on…», showing disappointment.
exercise: Nu is the 13th letter of which alphabet? | Russian | Arabic | *Greek
exercise: Grandma says «Nu?» while you are still putting on your coat. What is she asking? | Please be quiet | *Well? Come on! | The number thirteen | A kind of petrol station
exercise: Some brands write «nu-look» on their products. What does the playful spelling most likely mean? | a number | *new | a noisy sound
test: The small letter ν looks most like which English letter? | *v | n | u | m
test: Dad says «nu» with a long sigh when you stop to tie your shoes. How does he feel? | Sleepy | *Disappointed and impatient | Proud | Very excited
exam: Which of these is NOT a meaning of nu? | A Greek letter | *A type of dog | A Yiddish word meaning well? or come on | A playful spelling of new
